WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.730
- I've been telling this joke

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:05.120
at my stand-up which I told on,

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.670
I don't know how dirty we can get.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.107
- You can get as dirty as you want.

00:00:08.107 --> 00:00:12.250
(serene electronic music)

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.110
This is "The Rubin Report"
and I'm still Dave Rubin.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:15.040
Before we do anything else,

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.647
today, we have officially launched

00:00:16.647 --> 00:00:19.400
"The Rubin Report" community
in the Apple App Store,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.480
Google Play, and at RubinReport.com.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.940
But more importantly than that,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.810
joining me today is comedian, podcaster,

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:30.480
and now star of the new
documentary "No Safe Spaces",

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.670
which is in theaters as of today

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.080
where we're taping this
right now, Adam Carolla.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.150
Welcome to "The Rubin Report".

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.110
- Thank you, Dave.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.330
- Good to have you here.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:39.163
- Yeah.
- You do a lot of this--

00:00:39.163 --> 00:00:41.100
- I'm a fan of yours.
- Well, I'm a fan of yours,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.270
but you do a lot of this the other way.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.250
You interview people all the time.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.290
- I do, and I enjoy being interviewed.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:48.803
- [Dave] Which do you like more?

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.390
- It's like, well, what do
you like more: pizza or sushi?

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:58.090
Well, when you eat a metric
ton of pizza every month,

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.500
you're dying for a California roll.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.660
- [Dave] So this is your California roll?

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.137
- Yeah, it's not that one
is better than the other,

00:01:04.137 --> 00:01:07.890
it's like all I do is
interview, interview, interview,

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.150
and I like it when it's swapped.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.230
I also had this revelation

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.420
the other day which is I
used to say to Dr. Drew,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.660
what you do is easy,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.610
when we do these shows,
we'd be guests on shows,

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.630
and we do college campuses,
and we do whatever.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.550
And I realized when I had to be funny,

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.180
it was a calorie burner.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.330
And so what happened was,

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.410
but my comparison or contrast was this:

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:43.410
I've done a million radio,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.450
25 stations, you're
gonna talk to 25 stations

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:49.970
over the next three hours,

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.720
and it's a press junket yeah.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.720
And when I do one for my
book or a comedy that I'm in

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.250
or something of a comedic nature,

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.500
at the end of the three hours,

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.990
I'm exhausted because I'm trying
to be funny the whole time

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.790
because it's a comedy book,

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.800
I'm telling 'em jokes in the book,

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.340
I want them to think I'm
funny so the book is funny.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.230
- You gotta keep yourself entertained too.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.150
- Yes, although I don't
care about myself that much,

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.880
but yes, I do,

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.410
but the last one I did
was over documentary,

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:28.410
not "No Safe Spaces" but
my "Ford v Ferrari" doc

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:32.030
which was Shelby American
and the 24-hour war,

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.890
and all I was doing is
talking cars the whole time

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.550
and shopped the whole time,

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.610
and I was fresh as a daisy
after the three hours

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.300
because I realized I was
talking about stuff I knew,

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.730
whereas Dr. Drew gets
to talk about medicine.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.400
If we just talked about carpentry or cars,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:51.710
I wouldn't be exhausted.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:55.590
If I'm spinning the comedic
plates, it gets tiring.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.250
- What level of energy
do you have right now

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.350
for me to gauge how we should
do this interview then?

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:02.833
- I never wanna disappoint you,

00:03:05.737 --> 00:03:09.760
or your fans or your
viewers, so I'm putting out.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:10.593
- All right.

00:03:10.593 --> 00:03:12.060
Well, we'll do a little
bit on the stuff you know

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.990
and a little bit on the
stuff you pretend to know.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:14.823
How about that?

00:03:14.823 --> 00:03:15.897
- All up to you.

00:03:15.897 --> 00:03:17.980
- But you kinda do do a zillion things

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.260
because you consider yourself
a comic first, right?

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:22.093
- Yes.

00:03:22.093 --> 00:03:23.660
- So you're a comic, you're a podcaster,

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.660
documentaries, reality shows.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:30.063
Do you enjoy them all the same
or it's just whatever you do?

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.513
- I wanna do whatever's next.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.720
I think what makes a bad job

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.040
for a bad job is monotony, repetition.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.310
If you have a job where you go

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:49.070
I work at a medium-sized
technology company

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:51.910
and you go, okay, that sounds okay.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:52.890
But if you said to someone,

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.390
I have a job where I
work at a conveyor belt

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.620
and I just put the heads on
dolls and they just keep,

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.390
it sounds horrible
because it's the monotony.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:04.050
So if you think about it,

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:08.340
even hosting a late-night
show for 30 years

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:13.153
is still monotony within a good job.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:17.810
What most people don't
like is when they go,

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:21.350
he went to the same postal sorting center

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.160
in Arleta for 50 years and then he died,

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.203
everyone's feelings goes,
well, what a boring life,

00:04:26.203 --> 00:04:27.410
what a crappy life.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:30.300
So for me, doing a car doc,

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.163
doing a free-speech doc, doing a podcast,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.610
writing a book, being in a
TV show or whatever it is,

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.010
it's all just variety and
that's all I wanna do.

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:42.843
- So we're obviously

00:04:42.843 --> 00:04:45.400
gonna talk a lot about "No Safe Spaces",

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.580
but your podcast, you were
early in on the podcast game,

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:50.610
and your podcast,

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:55.383
is it basically the biggest
talk comedy podcast?

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.970
You and Rogen, it's hard
to tell who's bigger.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:00.825
Do you know or are you
measuring that everyday?

00:05:00.825 --> 00:05:02.600
- I don't know.
- Looking at the numbers?

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.520
- He's bigger on iTunes

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.780
but we don't get so much of
our listeners from iTunes,

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:08.670
so I don't know.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:12.383
I'll just say he's bigger,
I'll be magnanimous.

00:05:16.433 --> 00:05:20.300
We're the Guinness Book of
World Records downloaded,

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.360
so that much I know.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:23.717
That was some years ago so.

00:05:23.717 --> 00:05:25.040
- Do you remember the moment

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.420
when you wanted to make that
pivot to the digital thing?

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:30.470
'Cause I saw a lot of comics
get all kinds of crossed-up,

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:32.010
people that had really promising careers

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.050
that still want it to go
into TV or traditional radio,

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:36.510
and then a lot other
guys were like no, no,

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:37.600
there's something else brewing here,

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.450
and then they really
started flourishing online.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:43.963
I think I went that route,
which is what we're doing here.

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:48.980
- I had a terrestrial
radio job for many years,

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:53.160
maybe almost 15 years

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.740
of terrestrial radio straight through

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.023
that ended abruptly.

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:04.950
I was only interested in
keeping a communication line

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:08.440
between me and my audience,

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:12.840
and not all of my audience,
just some of my audience.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.110
I was always a big fan of talk radio,

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:18.040
I was appreciated the format.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:21.150
I always liked the connection

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:25.740
that I felt like the listeners
were having with the hosts.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.610
I always knew it was deeper

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:30.510
because there was a sense of loss

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:34.670
that transcended your favorite
sitcom going off the air,

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:37.580
last episode of "Friends",
last episode of "Seinfeld".

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.270
People are like, oh, last
episode of "Breaking Bad".

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:41.180
I wish it was still in the air

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:45.750
but when somebody like
Howard Stern hangs it up

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.320
or folks of that ilk,

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.340
there's gonna be a big
sense of loss for people,

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:55.750
and not just entertainment
loss, emotional loss.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:59.410
So I had been doing "Loveline"

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:00.550
and doing a morning show

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:02.490
with no commercials and a lot of talk,

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:07.180
and I felt like I had an
audience and I had a connection.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:12.180
And when my show ceased on a Friday,

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:17.470
I just said, to whoever is listening,

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:20.890
if you wanna come with
me to the digital world,

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:22.390
you may find me.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:25.570
And I'm assuming most of you aren't

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:28.290
because you're in the car
and it's going to dial,

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:30.030
and you're being passive.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:32.490
And so if you're not,
you're not, that's fine,

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:34.300
but that doesn't mean you need me,

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:35.870
it means you don't care that much.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:40.870
But for the few that do wanna
keep going down this road,

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:45.433
I can be found on the
internet, come Monday.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:48.730
We won't skip a day.

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:50.310
I'll stop on a Friday,

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:53.850
you'll have me waiting for you on Monday.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.410
I'm not sure what form exactly yet

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:57.920
but you will get me

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:01.090
and then we will try to
move forward from there.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.860
And that was really my only concern.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:07.820
There was no monetization
that I was aware of back then.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.530
It was just let's keep the connection

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.633
because it does feel like a death.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:17.290
And I know from listening to
talk radio as a young person,

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:20.180
when somebody finally signs
off or goes off the air,

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:22.281
I'm like, ah, I liked that guy.

00:08:22.281 --> 00:08:23.330
- (laughs) Right, right, right there.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:24.660
And in the old days' radio,

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:26.958
you were just gone at that point.

00:08:26.958 --> 00:08:28.300
You were just gone.
- You would've just been gone.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:33.230
So I have not missed a day since that date

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:38.200
which was I think the 23rd of February,

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:40.040
2009 or something.

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:41.760
- So you really were early on this,

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.740
and I remember the first
time I did your show

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:45.670
when I walked into your studio over there.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:46.740
I was like, holy cow.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:49.110
It was before I had my
home studio and everything.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:50.140
And I was like, holy cow,

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:53.400
this guy actually has
a building and staff,

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:54.730
and it's professional,

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:57.160
and there's an area to
hang out and coffee,

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.120
and all your posters and everything.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.020
I was like, oh, you can
really do this on your own,

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:06.070
which for me, it was like,
whoa, all right, great,

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:07.320
there's a path here.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:09.610
- Well, you're welcome.

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:12.360
- So thank you! (laughs)

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.560
- We'll clean that up in post.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.160
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, no post here.

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:17.900
See, I'm not as professional, no post.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.490
- We're supposed to borrow it.
- No post.

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:20.323
- All right.

00:09:20.323 --> 00:09:24.120
So I had this radio mind,

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.150
I had this thought where
you wanna do a show,

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:29.370
you need a producer if
you're doing a show,

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:31.550
you need a sound guy.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:35.640
You can't just completely mom-and-pop this

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:39.130
and expect to get the product

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:41.260
that you want day in and day out.

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:43.120
You want some production.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:48.120
So my earliest thoughts
were I didn't have,

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.330
like I'm betting on myself

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:54.510
or I know this is gonna be
bigger than Uber in five years,

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:55.810
or I didn't have any of that.

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:58.970
I just had I know how radio works,

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:01.700
I don't see why this is
different than radio,

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:04.890
I don't know why we would
treat it any different.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:07.570
We should have a staff,

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:09.950
we should have a studio,

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:12.380
the equipment should be good,

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:15.980
don't be scared to spend
some money to get money,

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:17.080
you're gonna have to spend it

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:20.180
to make some like any business,

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:22.820
and furthermore, I was like,

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:26.997
if clients are coming in
or potential supporters

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:32.980
or potential folks are gonna
buy ad time or whatever it is,

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:34.850
they should walk into a building

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:38.880
that looks like their
money's going somewhere.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:42.740
It's that thing of you
don't want your contractor,

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:44.209
and I know that's a sore subject today,

00:10:44.209 --> 00:10:46.777
but your contractor--

00:10:46.777 --> 00:10:48.500
- We're having a little
contracting issue at the moment.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:49.333
It's okay, it's okay.

00:10:49.333 --> 00:10:53.010
- Your contractor pulls up
in a total rust bucket beater

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.070
and then wants to charge you a premium,

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:58.719
you're like, look at your truck?

00:10:58.719 --> 00:10:59.552
You know what I mean?

00:10:59.552 --> 00:11:01.940
So my thing is get a nice truck,

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:04.650
get a nice bed box, get a lumber rack,

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:05.940
tuck your shirt in,

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:09.923
look like you're a good
contractor and then charge,

00:11:09.923 --> 00:11:12.030
then go ahead and gouge the elderly.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.751
- You're like the Jordan Peterson
of contractors, basically.

00:11:14.751 --> 00:11:15.584
- Right.

00:11:15.584 --> 00:11:16.417
- You're telling them to clean their room.

00:11:16.417 --> 00:11:18.140
You must be very impressed
with my lighting grid though.

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:18.973
- It is.

00:11:18.973 --> 00:11:19.806
- I mean, come on,

00:11:19.806 --> 00:11:21.870
that is a professional expensive
lighting grid right there.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:22.950
- It really is.

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:24.280
It really is nice.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:26.450
- You'll love this, the guy who
hung this whole entire grid,

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:27.750
which these are huge, you can imagine

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.750
how heavy all these
poles are and everything,

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:30.583
I didn't know who he was,

00:11:30.583 --> 00:11:32.187
I got referred to him by a friend.

00:11:32.187 --> 00:11:33.590
The guy shows up by that door,

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:35.800
he's about 60 years
old, I swear on my life,

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:36.950
his arm's in a sling,

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.590
he's wearing a Tommy Bahama
shirt and flip-flops,

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:41.780
he goes, "I'm Joe, I'm
here to hang the grid."

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:43.890
And he did this entire thing basically

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.230
with one arm and flip-flops,

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:47.740
15-foot high ceilings
with a system of pulleys

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:49.070
that he created with one arm.

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:50.710
- Wow!
- It was on a 15-foot ladder

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.060
with flip-flops, 60-year-old guy.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:53.140
It was incredible.

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:54.510
- People don't realize--

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:55.610
- So people work hard.

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:59.480
- Listen, I come from
the construction world

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:01.920
so I understand working hard,

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.360
and I also understand

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:07.350
the number one killer of
men over 60 are ladders.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:09.040
They--
- Ladders and flip-flops?

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:10.330
- Ladders and flip-flops.

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:13.870
It's not a good combination.

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:17.470
- All right, let's talk about
this movie, "No Safe Spaces"

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:19.230
'cause I felt, when I watched
it a couple weeks ago,

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:20.430
I went to the premiere here in L.A.,

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.990
and you were there and you
did this with Dennis Prager,

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:24.640
and Jordan Peterson was in it,

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.010
and Lindsay Shepherd was in it,

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:27.040
and Bret Weinstein was in it,

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:28.190
and Heather Heying was in it,

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:30.540
basically, I felt it was like
a Rubin Report reunion show.

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:31.500
I was very pleased.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.930
But there was one guy in it

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:34.600
that I thought was really incredible,

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:36.540
and I wanna just throw it to a clip.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:38.600
There's a reason that every time

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:41.010
one of these professors or TAs,

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.970
whether it's Lindsay Shepherd in Canada

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:45.450
or Bret Weinstein in Washington,

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:49.200
why are they all Lefties
who then say one thing

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.050
that upsets the Left
and then they're purged?

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:54.070
It will come for you.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.020
If there's someone that's
watching this right now

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:57.610
that is a hardcore progressive,

00:12:57.610 --> 00:12:59.360
that's going, man, I
hate Prager and Rubin,

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:01.120
and this is all nonsense, guess what?

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.780
If you have any spark
of individualism in you,

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:07.240
if you have anything about you

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:08.630
that's interesting or different,

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.100
they will come to destroy that too.

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:12.320
That guy is incredible.

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:14.021
- Yeah, he's got it.

00:13:14.021 --> 00:13:15.840
- It? It!

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.010
- I'm sure they're gonna spin that guy off

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:19.490
and do his own series.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:21.050
There's no way.

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:22.460
They continued down this path

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:24.570
with Prager and Corolla
when they got that.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:25.740
- Yeah.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:27.440
Why did you do this movie?

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:28.790
Where'd this all come from?

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:34.823
- Contrary to popular anything, for me,

00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:40.160
as we're discussing off the
air, I like experiences.

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:43.580
And the answer is usually yes.

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:47.480
People say to me, why did you
do "Dancing with the Stars"?

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:49.670
I'm like, "They asked."

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:52.313
And like, "Yeah, but what
made you want to do?"

00:13:52.313 --> 00:13:54.600
And I go, "'Cause they asked."

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.570
and I just went, oh, well, actually,

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:57.640
with "Dancing with the Stars",

00:13:57.640 --> 00:13:58.960
they asked me if I wanted to do it

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:00.800
and I thought, oh, I'm scared.

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:02.503
My first impulse was like,

00:14:04.053 --> 00:14:07.700
it's like Bobby Hinton wants
to see you in the alley

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:08.970
after the seventh grade

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.280
and you went oh, oh my god, I'm scared!

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:12.450
I'm scared and then you realized,

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:14.610
oh, I have to fight 'cause I'm scared.

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:15.970
That's what I felt.

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:18.230
And I like Prager.

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:21.670
I've had a chance to travel
around, play some colleges,

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:23.683
or do some events with him.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:27.710
Prager is a great guy
just to kick around with

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:32.710
'cause he has all the
wisdom and the knowledge

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:37.510
but in this jovial Uncle Joe

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:41.540
from Petticoat Junction fun way

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:45.000
but also is this fountain
of wisdom and knowledge

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.113
and there's really no better guy.

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:53.420
I have been lucky in that

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:58.560
hanging out with Jimmy Kimmel,

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:01.040
hanging out with Dr. Drew,

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:02.800
hanging out with Dennis Prager,

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.070
just sitting next to 'em on a long flight

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:05.903
or something like,

00:15:05.903 --> 00:15:10.520
there's no better guys
to just talk or laugh

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:12.490
or exchange ideas with.

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:15.370
All different but they're great.

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:16.840
And I love that.

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:20.500
I love sitting with someone
and just hearing their ideas

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:22.270
and sharing ideas.

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:25.630
So when they said we wanna do this movie,

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:27.940
and Dennis Prager's doing it,

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:29.690
I was just kinda like, oh, good!

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:33.150
More time to hang out with Dennis Prager.

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:34.423
I like the theme.

00:15:37.569 --> 00:15:40.790
If you say to me we
want you to be involved

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:43.690
with this product or project

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:46.100
and we're not gonna tell you what to say,

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:49.860
you give your opinions
on stuff, I'm usually in.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:53.710
I'm usually in if you go
say whatever you want.

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:56.010
If you say we're doing this project,

00:15:56.010 --> 00:15:59.750
here's a script, memorize
it, regurgitate it,

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:02.420
I'm usually like, ah, I'm
not that interested in that.

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:04.780
But you're gonna sit with Prager,

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.470
we're gonna bring up subjects,

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:10.720
you guys are gonna
tag-team those subjects.

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:12.800
And I'm like, oh, I'm in.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:17.800
- So the movie mostly is about
the college campus upheaval

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:18.910
that's going on right now.

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:21.060
Do you remember when you first were aware

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:23.040
that there was the problem?

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:25.700
- I was hearing from other comedians

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:28.973
that they didn't wanna
go to college campuses,

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:32.690
like anyone seeing in the news

00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:35.770
when people were going to
speak at college campuses

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:37.610
and unable to speak,

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:42.610
I live in this world where
it's mostly comedy world.

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:47.010
So I have this bizarre world

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:50.660
where I'm good friends
with Sarah Silverman

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:52.680
but I'm also friends with Ben Shapiro.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:56.000
So I would hear these bits and pieces,

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:58.683
and people saying to me,

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:02.870
I remember backstage of The Comedy Store,

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:05.227
Sarah said to me,

00:17:05.227 --> 00:17:08.940
"Your joke, that joke,
that black joke you have,"

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:10.987
she's like, "So funny.

00:17:10.987 --> 00:17:12.540
"Too bad you can't do it."

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:15.530
And I was like, oh, that sucked.

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:18.450
It's weird that comedians are
saying to other comedians,

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:19.630
like, hey, it's a great joke,

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:20.840
too bad you can't do it.

00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:23.180
- What do you really make of that though?

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:25.170
So I'm not a fan of Sarah's.

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:27.170
I've invited her on the show many times.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.100
I don't wanna make this about
your friends specifically

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:31.940
so you don't have to
answer directly to her,

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:34.390
but there's a series of comics now

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:36.720
that were really politically
incorrect back in the day.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:38.903
She's been in blackface, right?

00:17:38.903 --> 00:17:40.630
Jimmy Kimmel's been in blackface.

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:42.470
I don't think either
one of them are racist

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:44.650
but by the rules that the Left is setting

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:48.100
which they are about those rules,

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:49.760
now she's saying to you,

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:51.830
oh, don't make that black joke anymore,

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:54.060
and there's a lot of that
happening with comics,

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:56.480
and I never would have
expected that with comedians.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:59.153
- Well, if you think about comedians,

00:18:02.366 --> 00:18:06.040
(laughs) the thing about comedians,

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:08.593
at their core, they wanna be loved.

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:12.798
You're standing on stage
saying like, please.

00:18:12.798 --> 00:18:13.930
- [Dave] Love me!

00:18:13.930 --> 00:18:16.230
- Please love me, you know what I mean?

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:18.530
They weren't the most popular kids,

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:20.363
they weren't jocks in high school.

00:18:21.770 --> 00:18:24.930
If you think about why are comedians,

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:28.493
they're very sensitive
which is counterintuitive.

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:31.687
Jeff Ross, The Roastmaster,

00:18:31.687 --> 00:18:33.720
he's a super sensitive guy.

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:35.700
And I don't mean that in a weak way

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:40.130
but he's nothing like The Roastmaster.

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:42.810
He's a sweet, sensitive guy.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:45.220
Many comedians are sensitive.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:47.320
Many comedians wanna be loved.

00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:49.050
Many actors wanna be loved.

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:50.150
That's the profession.

00:18:50.150 --> 00:18:55.150
They're not taking the job
of staff sergeant or foreman.

00:18:58.336 --> 00:19:00.320
They have a job that goes
like, please accept me,

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:01.460
please love me,

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:05.480
so when these matters come down the pike,

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:07.800
they're, oh, what would get people

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:10.550
to love me and accept me and embrace me

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:13.670
and send out positive
tweets and vibes and stuff?

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:18.670
So they are wrestling with this duality

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:22.530
of I speak my mind, I tell it like it is,

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:25.620
I take no prisoners, please
love me, please love me!

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:27.510
- Yeah, that's a freaking fine line.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:29.840
- That's a pretty weird fine line.

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:33.460
My thing has always been

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:38.840
I know I'm not an asshole

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:41.963
so I am now free to act like an asshole.

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.440
My whole thing is I know
I'm not a bad person

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:47.890
so I can say whatever I want.

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:52.593
It's basically this.

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:58.730
If you have friends who
you know they love you

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:01.010
and you know you love them,

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:04.250
then you let the ballbusting begin.

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:09.250
If you have somebody, if you
think about it psychologically,

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:12.320
there's that person.

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:14.970
There's always that one person at work

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:18.370
where there's five of
you wanna go to lunch,

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:20.540
and then there's Andy,

00:20:20.540 --> 00:20:24.500
and you don't really want
Andy to come with you

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:27.200
but if Andy ever catches wind,

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:29.370
you have to overcompensate,

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:31.220
like, oh, Andy, come on, dude!

00:20:31.220 --> 00:20:32.550
And then he's going, I don't know,

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:34.017
I think I'm just gonna eat at my desk.

00:20:34.017 --> 00:20:34.850
And everyone goes,

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:36.650
it wouldn't be lunch without you, brother!

00:20:36.650 --> 00:20:37.680
And it's like what they're doing

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:40.360
is they all feel weird and bad
for not wanting to be there

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:42.970
that they actually go the other way.

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:47.820
But if you love Andy and
Andy loves you, then you go,

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:49.570
we'll get you a burger and bring it back.

00:20:49.570 --> 00:20:51.120
Maybe I'll take a bite out of it,

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:52.100
and then he laughs, and then you laugh,

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:54.150
and then he ends up going.

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:57.330
But I feel that way about making fun

00:20:57.330 --> 00:21:02.330
of groups or races or
individuals or anything.

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:07.500
You have some darkness in your heart.

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:12.200
I always say you know how
you know I'm not racist?

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:14.520
I make so many racist jokes.

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:15.700
If I was racist, I'd be like,

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:18.860
I don't make anymore racist
jokes, then we're onto you.

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:21.130
- Do you think there's some
element of it though with comics

00:21:21.130 --> 00:21:23.850
where it's like the ones that have done

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:25.290
some of that stuff in the past,

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:27.720
now, it's like they have to pay penance

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:30.230
to make sure that their
past doesn't destroy them,

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:31.840
where you're saying you're guilt-free

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:33.320
because you've done some stuff.

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:34.153
Do you ever worry

00:21:34.153 --> 00:21:36.990
that they're gonna go back
in "The Man Show" clips

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:40.090
and be, oh, he hates women
or any of this kinda stuff,

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:42.550
but what they're doing
is a more dangerous game

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:44.210
because again, I don't wanna make it

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:45.610
about those two people specifically

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:48.610
but there's a series of
comics now and public people

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:50.690
that are calling everyone
racists and bigots

00:21:50.690 --> 00:21:52.311
and all the Conservatives are bad guys.

00:21:52.311 --> 00:21:53.580
You know what I'm talking about, right?

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:56.973
And it's like, well, you
guys used to do that stuff.

00:21:58.399 --> 00:22:00.560
It strikes me as just it's
just self-preservation.

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:02.550
I get the emotionally damaged part

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:05.870
but it just strikes me as
it's just business in a way.

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:10.870
- Well, look, it is a business in that.

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:19.560
It's a business where we
don't need Matt Lauer.

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:20.680
- Let me put it like this.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:22.210
Sorry to interrupt, but
let me put it this way.

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:23.140
So if you showed up,

00:22:23.140 --> 00:22:24.630
if there was a thing that 20 years ago,

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:26.100
you showed up in blackface,

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:28.490
I don't think you would get
the pass that Kimmel's gotten

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:30.050
because Kimmel is a big Lefty now,

00:22:30.050 --> 00:22:32.690
he's a Hollywood Lefty, so he gets a pass,

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:35.900
where you're more libertarian Conservative

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:37.950
or just out there.

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:40.700
Now, you're also
self-funded so that helps.

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:43.110
You're your own boss so that's good

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:45.683
but you wouldn't get the
pass, do you agree with that?

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:48.570
- Here's my take on it.

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:53.420
I think they go where the getting's good.

00:22:53.420 --> 00:22:56.360
So they're basically feral cats

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:59.830
looking for a saucer of milk.

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:02.790
And if you don't put it out on the porch,

00:23:02.790 --> 00:23:04.760
they'll keep going.

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:06.730
People don't really bother me

00:23:06.730 --> 00:23:09.800
even though I say horrible things,

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:12.040
and not horrible things by my definition

00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:14.090
but by their definition.

00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:17.770
I do have a picture of me in blackface.

00:23:17.770 --> 00:23:22.590
It's going as Mr. T for Halloween in 1985.

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:25.160
I put it out there.

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:26.910
I'm like, look, listen,

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:29.860
this isn't blackface, this is Mr. T.

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:31.240
That's not blackface.

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:32.623
That's a minstrel show.

00:23:32.623 --> 00:23:37.623
This is me dressing like Mr. T
because I'm a fan of Mr. T's.

00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:39.623
So this is a--

00:23:39.623 --> 00:23:41.730
- Which that's the normal, thoughtful way

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:42.930
of dealing with this, of course.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:44.340
You weren't doing it to mock the guy,

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:46.510
you were doing it out
of admiration and love.

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:47.343
- Right.

00:23:47.343 --> 00:23:49.160
And you would like to take it

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:52.070
and contort it and twist it into blackface

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:55.200
which is general generic

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:59.140
make fun of black people vaudeville stuff.

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:00.210
This is not that.

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:01.370
So first things first,

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:05.450
I'm not signing off on your
definition of what I did.

00:24:05.450 --> 00:24:08.030
I dressed up as Mr. T.

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:11.710
Mr. T is black, I went with a black face,

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:15.600
which is not blackface,
it's me dressing as Mr. T.

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:19.890
And furthermore, I'm not
apologizing to anybody about it.

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:23.620
And if you talk to Mr. T,
I bet he would be happy

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:27.180
that a bunch of kids
dressed like him in 1985

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:28.940
and said, "I pity the fool."

00:24:28.940 --> 00:24:31.262
over and over again at
a Halloween party so.

00:24:31.262 --> 00:24:32.800
- I used to buy Mr. T cereal.

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:36.447
That's far worse than
doing blackface of Mr. T.

00:24:36.447 --> 00:24:38.220
- You consumed Mr. T.

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:40.743
So I think with me,

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:45.450
there's no job to remove me from per se.

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:48.413
And then also, I don't apologize.

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:53.840
The other thing is I don't let

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:58.840
or I attempt not to let
people create this narrative

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.330
and create this theme

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:07.330
which is there's a
picture of you with black,

00:25:07.470 --> 00:25:08.503
it wasn't paint.

00:25:11.488 --> 00:25:15.530
I think I took a toilet paper
roll and burnt the end of it

00:25:15.530 --> 00:25:18.180
and then smeared that soot on my face.

00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:21.950
But there's a picture of you in blackface,

00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:25.090
and I go no, no, there's a
picture of me dressed like Mr. T.

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:26.240
And they go, in blackface.

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:29.030
I go, not blackface,
that's a different genre.

00:25:29.030 --> 00:25:30.410
This is Mr. T.

00:25:30.410 --> 00:25:32.650
That's from the '40s or the '30s.

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:34.160
This is from '80s.

00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:35.120
But you still.

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:36.793
I go, I was dressed as Mr. T.

00:25:37.660 --> 00:25:39.290
It's an homage to the man.

00:25:39.290 --> 00:25:40.620
And what do you want me to do about it?

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:43.060
I'll put every picture up on
the internet, I don't care.

00:25:43.060 --> 00:25:43.893
- Yeah.

00:25:43.893 --> 00:25:46.920
Are you fascinated how it hits every comic

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:48.840
no matter how vanilla they are?

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:49.716
There's a moment,

00:25:49.716 --> 00:25:51.820
it's in the movie about
Seinfeld telling this story

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:53.390
about doing a college gig

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:55.220
and he does a joke about, what is it,

00:25:55.220 --> 00:25:57.150
a gay British king or something?

00:25:57.150 --> 00:25:58.110
- [Adam] Maybe a French king.

00:25:58.110 --> 00:25:59.820
- A French King with a loose hand

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:01.640
and he said he did this joke

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:04.460
and the kids basically,
it's like five years ago,

00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:06.940
the kids basically groaned.

00:26:06.940 --> 00:26:10.360
And it's Seinfeld who I
love Jerry's stand-up,

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:11.840
the show is the greatest sitcom ever.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:12.673
It's like I don't know

00:26:12.673 --> 00:26:14.480
any of his political beliefs on anything.

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:18.240
He is so guarded what his
actual political beliefs are,

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:20.950
and he does this silly joke
about a French king's hand

00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:24.120
and that's even too politically incorrect

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:25.200
for these students.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:27.313
Yeah, it's funny.

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:32.070
It's kinda interesting

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:36.210
'cause I do know a lot of these comedians

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:41.210
and we all know our actors,
our Hollywood community,

00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:45.410
I've now figured it out
where you went like,

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:47.480
well, I know Sarah Silverman

00:26:47.480 --> 00:26:49.700
and Mark Ruffalo and Alyssa Milano,

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:50.533
I know their politics,

00:26:50.533 --> 00:26:53.790
and you go, but I don't know
Jerry Seinfeld's politics,

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:55.550
or I don't know Jay Leno's politics.

00:26:55.550 --> 00:26:56.491
I go--
- Ah, now I--

00:26:56.491 --> 00:26:59.367
- My new law and order is
just when you don't know.

00:26:59.367 --> 00:27:01.290
- Oh!
- Go ahead and check

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:04.410
the Republican box because my new theory,

00:27:04.410 --> 00:27:05.710
I started thinking about,

00:27:05.710 --> 00:27:08.637
and I have some insider
trading information here.

00:27:08.637 --> 00:27:11.100
I'm like, how come everyone else

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:15.635
is out there blasting it out
from the highest mountain?

00:27:15.635 --> 00:27:18.970
When you're on the Left

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:23.970
and you're not playing it close
to the vest like a Morano.

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:28.520
It's a Dennis Prager reference.

00:27:28.520 --> 00:27:31.880
But the the Jews of Spain who are like,

00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:33.423
don't let anyone find,

00:27:35.644 --> 00:27:40.020
if you are backing Bernie or
Elizabeth Warren or whatever

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:42.180
and you're in the Hollywood community,

00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:44.840
you have a bullhorn and
you're on a mountaintop.

00:27:44.840 --> 00:27:49.380
So if you really just
kinda do the reverse math,

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:51.020
who are the people

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:54.030
whose politics you're
completely unaware of

00:27:54.030 --> 00:27:57.840
and you've never heard
'em speak a word about it,

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:01.830
you must go, hmm, how
come I've never heard,

00:28:01.830 --> 00:28:04.540
how come I've never seen 'em in a rally,

00:28:04.540 --> 00:28:08.650
a women's rally or marching on
or sending a tweet out about,

00:28:08.650 --> 00:28:13.650
and you go, I'm gonna do
a reverse math on that.

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:16.870
- So you're saying Seinfeld
ain't voting for Bernie.

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:21.000
- I think the guys who
you hear nothing about

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:26.670
have a leaning that's
probably a little less Bernie

00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:29.690
and probably a little more Trump.

00:28:29.690 --> 00:28:33.410
- Do you miss the days when not
everything was so political?

00:28:33.410 --> 00:28:34.847
'Cause that also is
what this movie is about

00:28:34.847 --> 00:28:36.510
and that politics has now leaked

00:28:36.510 --> 00:28:38.430
into every part of society all the time.

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:40.100
So it's like you just mentioned Leno

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:41.603
and it's like back in the day,

00:28:42.464 --> 00:28:43.510
I never really liked Leno

00:28:43.510 --> 00:28:45.070
'cause I didn't know what
he thought about anything.

00:28:45.070 --> 00:28:47.720
And in a weird way, I miss that now.

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:49.440
I don't watch any of
those Late Night shows,

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:52.430
but every night, when you
see clips on Twitter of,

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:53.670
I can't even think of who's the host.

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:55.079
Who's the--

00:28:55.079 --> 00:28:55.948
- Fallon, Jimmy Kimmel.
- Fallon,

00:28:55.948 --> 00:28:56.886
and who's the other one?

00:28:56.886 --> 00:28:58.240
- Colbert.
- The NBC one?

00:28:58.240 --> 00:28:59.520
The other guy.

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:00.540
- [Both] Seth Meyers.

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:03.280
- It's like they're just railing on Trump.

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:06.930
It's the same thing
every night over and over

00:29:06.930 --> 00:29:09.420
where it's so political all the time,

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:11.100
and it's made me miss the day

00:29:11.100 --> 00:29:13.241
that Leno would just go
up there and (mumbles),

00:29:13.241 --> 00:29:15.130
and it was just like he
was just doing this thing

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:17.540
that was for everybody.

00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:18.373
- Yeah.

00:29:18.373 --> 00:29:23.160
I think there's an element
of a saturation point.

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:28.350
You did want Leno back in the day

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:31.610
to dig into more substantive issues

00:29:31.610 --> 00:29:36.380
or reveal a little more
of himself and all that.

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:39.750
And Jay, who I know really well,

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:42.970
is always realized that

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:46.710
this is a marathon

00:29:46.710 --> 00:29:48.650
and it's not a sprint.

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:49.580
- I respect that now.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:51.450
I regret I was hard on him for years

00:29:51.450 --> 00:29:54.290
when I was doing standup,
and I regret it actually.

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:59.290
- And I think he's realized that no good

00:29:59.590 --> 00:30:01.233
could come from him.

00:30:04.580 --> 00:30:09.580
I believe there's some
old-school comedians who go,

00:30:09.870 --> 00:30:12.096
you're not supposed to know me.

00:30:12.096 --> 00:30:14.520
You're supposed to know my jokes.

00:30:14.520 --> 00:30:16.760
I'm gonna let my jokes do my talking.

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:19.280
I don't want the distraction

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:23.190
of you knowing I'm Republican or Democrat,

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:24.710
or gay or straight, or anything.

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:26.360
I don't want anything bleeding

00:30:26.360 --> 00:30:30.500
into these jokes I'm gonna lay over you.

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:35.500
But also, when you take a stand

00:30:39.098 --> 00:30:43.180
as a Patriots fan and
you let the world know

00:30:43.180 --> 00:30:44.800
you're the number one Patriot fan.

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:48.453
The Patriots are capable of
then spending a decade lose it.

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:54.500
Why declare a major when the
next person could get voted in

00:30:54.560 --> 00:30:57.880
and this thing could swing
like the other direction?

00:30:57.880 --> 00:31:00.380
It seems like you're putting

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:03.000
your career needlessly in jeopardy.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:04.570
- That's crazy for a comic,

00:31:04.570 --> 00:31:07.230
that truth-telling thing
and the like thing,

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:10.130
they can often just be
at odds with each other.

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:11.559
- Yeah.

00:31:11.559 --> 00:31:14.690
I do think that many people,

00:31:14.690 --> 00:31:16.160
many comedians, as you know,

00:31:16.160 --> 00:31:20.657
just have a different approach to comedy.

00:31:23.075 --> 00:31:27.870
And also, there's an element
of you live in Hollywood,

00:31:27.870 --> 00:31:29.763
you have a job,

00:31:32.710 --> 00:31:34.760
I've been telling this joke at my standup

00:31:34.760 --> 00:31:37.340
which I told on,

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:38.930
I don't know how dirty we can get.

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:40.240
- You can get as dirty as you want.

00:31:40.240 --> 00:31:43.080
- I told this joke when
we were doing the--

00:31:43.080 --> 00:31:44.421
- You wanna get in
blackface for this one or?

00:31:44.421 --> 00:31:45.496
- With a roast.

00:31:45.496 --> 00:31:46.329
(both laughing)

00:31:46.329 --> 00:31:47.250
- I got a guy with some
charcoal back there.

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:52.250
- We were doing a roast on
Comedy Central for Alec Baldwin.

00:31:56.370 --> 00:31:58.860
Larry David was sitting in the front row,

00:31:58.860 --> 00:32:02.017
and he literally on the commercial
break came over and went,

00:32:02.017 --> 00:32:03.283
"That's the funniest
thing I've ever heard."

00:32:03.283 --> 00:32:05.880
That was the greatest moment
of my life 'cause I love--

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:07.040
- That's pretty.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:08.680
- [Both] Pretty, pretty good.

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:09.593
- He literally, then I was like,

00:32:09.593 --> 00:32:12.290
he was like, "That joke, I love it."

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:14.493
It's a very Larry David joke,

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:18.900
and it's also totally
politically incorrect.

00:32:18.900 --> 00:32:23.900
But it is here to illustrate
that when I was a carpenter,

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:29.320
or guys who do fabrication work

00:32:29.320 --> 00:32:31.310
and can shape metal into any shape,

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:33.110
or a guy in flip-flops with one arm

00:32:33.110 --> 00:32:35.160
who can hang a lighting grid,

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:37.640
it doesn't really matter
what his politics are,

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:39.460
we can't run 'em out.

00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:41.870
We can't go, no more
lighting grid work for you,

00:32:41.870 --> 00:32:44.340
or hey man, if you're
a journeyman carpenter,

00:32:44.340 --> 00:32:48.400
if you're a good carpenter,
you can't be drummed out.

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:50.800
There's too many people who need you.

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:52.720
If you're an actor,

00:32:52.720 --> 00:32:56.230
look, Matt Lauer made $30 million a year,

00:32:56.230 --> 00:32:57.850
now Matt Lauer makes nothing.

00:32:57.850 --> 00:32:59.378
Does anyone miss Matt Lauer?

00:32:59.378 --> 00:33:00.211
- Right.

00:33:00.211 --> 00:33:01.810
- And we don't need Matt Lauer.

00:33:01.810 --> 00:33:03.390
We didn't need Billy Bush.

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:05.600
We can just knock out and
go with somebody else.

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:06.960
We don't need you.

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:09.120
And that's kinda scary.

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:12.917
You live in a town where
the journeyman carpenter,

00:33:12.917 --> 00:33:14.500
they don't have to worry
what they talk about.

00:33:14.500 --> 00:33:17.990
But an actor, you could get
tossed out of this club.

00:33:17.990 --> 00:33:22.990
And the joke was I had Alec
Baldwin sitting over here

00:33:24.610 --> 00:33:27.530
and I had Robert De Niro
sitting next to him,

00:33:27.530 --> 00:33:29.877
and I said, "Two of the
finest actors in America.

00:33:29.877 --> 00:33:34.877
"What amazing craft and an
ability, unbelievable ability.

00:33:35.337 --> 00:33:37.417
"Look at the two of you!

00:33:37.417 --> 00:33:39.083
"Maybe the two greatest ever.

00:33:39.083 --> 00:33:41.137
"I have so much respect for you."

00:33:43.570 --> 00:33:47.760
After hearing about the whole
Harvey Weinstein situation

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:49.350
and hearing a lot of those stories,

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:51.140
I don't know if I have such high respect

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:54.430
for the craft of acting anymore.

00:33:54.430 --> 00:33:57.030
What other job works this way?

00:33:57.030 --> 00:33:59.333
You wanna be a commercial airline pilot,

00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:01.353
blow that fat joke.

00:34:02.519 --> 00:34:05.480
They'll have you up in
the air this afternoon.

00:34:05.480 --> 00:34:07.590
You wanna be a dental hygienist?

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:08.520
Blow the fat joke,

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:10.094
more than likely, you'll start tomorrow.

00:34:10.094 --> 00:34:11.640
(Dave laughs)

00:34:11.640 --> 00:34:13.523
I was making the joke that
maybe acting is not as hard.

00:34:13.523 --> 00:34:15.780
- [Dave] I think that proves your point.

00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:16.613
- Right.

00:34:16.613 --> 00:34:21.310
So actors are in a precarious situation.

00:34:21.310 --> 00:34:25.240
Now, Dave Chappelle isn't.

00:34:25.240 --> 00:34:27.900
Dave Chappelle's a journeyman carpenter,

00:34:27.900 --> 00:34:30.680
but Matt Lauer reads a teleprompter,

00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:33.780
and many actors could be replaced by

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:34.970
whenever they do those things,

00:34:34.970 --> 00:34:38.790
or you know who was gonna play
the original Indiana Jones

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:40.450
and you go, oh, there was gonna be--

00:34:40.450 --> 00:34:41.283
- Nic Cage?
- I don't know,

00:34:41.283 --> 00:34:43.180
the original 007 was gonna be?

00:34:43.180 --> 00:34:45.150
Right, right, you're
gonna see and you realize,

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:46.160
they live in a world

00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:48.830
where someone else could just
take their job the next day

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:50.890
and we're not really gonna miss 'em,

00:34:50.890 --> 00:34:53.930
so you gotta follow the party line.

00:34:53.930 --> 00:34:55.100
- It's interesting that
you bring up Chappelle

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:56.840
because you're basically
saying a guy like him

00:34:56.840 --> 00:35:00.930
who has just relentlessly
fought the system,

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:03.400
his bravery is what now protects him.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:05.730
Which that is a theme
throughout the movie too,

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:07.740
that all of the people
that I mentioned earlier

00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:11.550
from Bret and Lindsay and
Jordan, who else is in there?

00:35:11.550 --> 00:35:12.920
- Oh, well--
- Everybody's in there.

00:35:12.920 --> 00:35:16.860
You had--
- But there's Van Jones.

00:35:16.860 --> 00:35:17.740
- Van Jones, yeah.

00:35:17.740 --> 00:35:20.700
- And there's Dershowitz.

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:22.860
- So that's interesting
'cause you guys make a point,

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:24.350
and this is the part that I'm in,

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:26.330
you make a point of saying

00:35:26.330 --> 00:35:29.070
that there are a couple Liberals left.

00:35:29.070 --> 00:35:31.560
Did you feel like that was a
point you really had to hit,

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:32.760
otherwise you would just be seen

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:35.480
as a couple good Liberals left,

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:36.620
as opposed to just saying,

00:35:36.620 --> 00:35:39.100
well then, we're just making
a Conservative movie here

00:35:39.100 --> 00:35:40.000
or something like?

00:35:41.985 --> 00:35:43.580
- I think there's a catch to it

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:45.343
where someone will say,

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:49.200
and then there's obviously
been this critique,

00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:54.170
you're only highlighting
Right-leaning speakers

00:35:54.170 --> 00:35:55.990
or Conservative speakers

00:35:55.990 --> 00:35:58.740
who are being shouted
off the college campuses.

00:35:58.740 --> 00:36:00.580
You're only highlighting

00:36:00.580 --> 00:36:02.840
the Ben Shapiros and the Ann Coulters.

00:36:02.840 --> 00:36:05.637
And the answer is 'cause
they don't shout off,

00:36:05.637 --> 00:36:07.530
and they don't shout
out Left-leaning ones.

00:36:07.530 --> 00:36:10.860
So this is a weird thing
you're asking us to do.

00:36:10.860 --> 00:36:12.640
Be more even-handed.

00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:15.340
Well, if Rachel Maddow went to Berkeley,

00:36:15.340 --> 00:36:17.850
there wouldn't be fires
in the parking lot.

00:36:17.850 --> 00:36:18.683
- Yeah.

00:36:18.683 --> 00:36:19.516
You mean it wouldn't cost, what was it?

00:36:19.516 --> 00:36:22.364
Was it three or 600,000 to
secure that Shapiro event?

00:36:22.364 --> 00:36:23.197
I believe--
- I think it was 600,000.

00:36:23.197 --> 00:36:24.030
- I think it was six.

00:36:24.030 --> 00:36:25.320
I think it was six.
- No, so,

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:27.430
this thing of we only highlight this.

00:36:27.430 --> 00:36:30.050
Well, it's like, those are the
only people who get protested

00:36:30.050 --> 00:36:32.610
so if you're gonna highlight protests,

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:35.310
then unfortunately, it's
only gonna be from that side.

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:39.730
We've had conversations.

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:41.650
We don't want this to come across

00:36:41.650 --> 00:36:44.950
as some Right-wing tutorial.

00:36:44.950 --> 00:36:45.783
- Yeah, and it does.

00:36:45.783 --> 00:36:49.510
I mean, it does not.
- We wanted the Van Joneses

00:36:49.510 --> 00:36:53.690
and the Cornel Wests and
the Alan Dershowitz' eye,

00:36:53.690 --> 00:36:58.690
and many people from all sides
and everything in between

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:01.750
to speak about this subject,

00:37:01.750 --> 00:37:03.573
and that was intentional.

00:37:05.620 --> 00:37:06.570
- A lot of this stuff

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:08.330
that's happening on colleges in the movie,

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:11.660
you link it to how we've raised kids now.

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:14.730
So you wanna kick the
Millennials while they're down?

00:37:14.730 --> 00:37:15.860
- I do.

00:37:15.860 --> 00:37:20.310
I do as I travel

00:37:20.310 --> 00:37:21.703
through this land.

00:37:23.270 --> 00:37:25.650
I think the self-esteem movement

00:37:27.218 --> 00:37:29.643
is really the genesis of this.

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:34.820
'Cause I started thinking, when I was 19,

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:39.640
I didn't have any self-esteem
and my thing was like,

00:37:39.640 --> 00:37:42.470
I'm not gonna tell an adult what to do.

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:43.320
I would never dream.

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:47.310
If I was walking down
the street and I was 16

00:37:47.310 --> 00:37:50.680
and I passed a 45-year-old
guy and he's going, hey, stop,

00:37:50.680 --> 00:37:51.950
and I'd went, well, I need to go,

00:37:51.950 --> 00:37:53.220
give me 20 push-ups,

00:37:53.220 --> 00:37:55.560
I'd be a little, okay, with diamond hands

00:37:55.560 --> 00:37:56.950
or we've gotta do a wide spread?

00:37:56.950 --> 00:37:58.290
I would have just done it

00:37:58.290 --> 00:38:00.530
because he's a dude and he's older than me

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:01.870
and he told me to do something.

00:38:01.870 --> 00:38:03.300
That's how I was wired.

00:38:03.300 --> 00:38:07.470
So I started thinking like,

00:38:07.470 --> 00:38:12.280
well, if I were 19 and I
was on a college campus,

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:14.110
and I was on a construction site,

00:38:14.110 --> 00:38:16.200
but if I was on a college campus.

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:18.080
And by the way, when I was
on a construction site,

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:20.737
I had a foreman go, he said,

00:38:20.737 --> 00:38:24.080
"Go to my truck, get my four-foot
level out of my gun rack."

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:26.450
He had a four-foot level
hanging on his gun rack.

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:28.110
And I said, "Okay."

00:38:28.110 --> 00:38:31.260
And I started walking to the
truck and he goes, "Hey, run."

00:38:31.260 --> 00:38:34.050
And I started running
'cause he told me to run,

00:38:34.050 --> 00:38:35.369
'cause he's older than I am.

00:38:35.369 --> 00:38:36.350
And before--
- And also that gun rack,

00:38:36.350 --> 00:38:38.370
so it was probably wiser to follow him.

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:39.581
- Yeah, where's the gun?

00:38:39.581 --> 00:38:40.414
(gun cocks)

00:38:40.414 --> 00:38:41.247
Run!

00:38:41.247 --> 00:38:42.380
- It probably was in all levels in their.

00:38:42.380 --> 00:38:44.000
- Run serpentine!

00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:47.210
So I started thinking,

00:38:47.210 --> 00:38:52.140
if I was 19 and I was on a college campus

00:38:52.140 --> 00:38:55.890
and they announced that some 50-year-old

00:38:55.890 --> 00:38:59.140
with a bunch of degrees
was coming to speak,

00:38:59.140 --> 00:39:02.580
I would never go, over my dead body.

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:05.370
I'd just be like, oh, okay, all right.

00:39:05.370 --> 00:39:07.820
And then someone would go,

00:39:07.820 --> 00:39:10.920
well, Adam, he's gonna talk
about stuff you disagree with.

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:13.280
And I'd go, well, he's 50
and has all the degrees

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:16.530
so I'm probably wrong.

00:39:16.530 --> 00:39:18.780
And then someone would go,

00:39:18.780 --> 00:39:20.657
dude, does he have the
right to speak here?

00:39:20.657 --> 00:39:23.623
And I go, of course, it's not my campus!

00:39:25.320 --> 00:39:28.790
Basically, this is a
four-year motel I'm living at.

00:39:28.790 --> 00:39:30.890
I don't own the building.

00:39:30.890 --> 00:39:31.723
Who?

00:39:31.723 --> 00:39:32.820
No, that's not for me to.

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:33.880
And then if they went,

00:39:33.880 --> 00:39:37.700
we're gonna go rally and make
'em create a human blockade.

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:39.820
I'd be like, why?

00:39:39.820 --> 00:39:42.170
Just don't go to the show!

00:39:42.170 --> 00:39:43.620
Well, I don't get it.

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:44.750
And I realize--

00:39:44.750 --> 00:39:46.740
- Sort of like a sane, functioning person,

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:47.573
that's what you're saying.

00:39:47.573 --> 00:39:48.406
You would have acted

00:39:48.406 --> 00:39:50.170
like a human should be.
- Yeah!

00:39:50.170 --> 00:39:55.170
Well, how high does your
self-esteem have to be to go,

00:39:55.270 --> 00:39:56.950
not on my watch,

00:39:56.950 --> 00:40:01.950
no way is David Rubin or,
I'm sorry, Jordan Rubin,

00:40:03.340 --> 00:40:05.740
or Ben Shapiro, sorry--

00:40:05.740 --> 00:40:06.639
- You're combining all these people.

00:40:06.639 --> 00:40:08.669
- I combined 'em into one being.

00:40:08.669 --> 00:40:11.120
- What a horrible transformer!

00:40:11.120 --> 00:40:13.883
- Yeah, that's gonna be protested.

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:19.670
Why would that be my job?

00:40:19.670 --> 00:40:21.280
Who am I to do that?

00:40:21.280 --> 00:40:24.070
So if you think about what that takes,

00:40:24.070 --> 00:40:27.210
that takes a life of grooming.

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:31.310
That takes a life of don't let
anyone tell you what to do.

00:40:31.310 --> 00:40:33.230
You tell them what to do.

00:40:33.230 --> 00:40:38.013
You're special, your opinion
matters, don't let anybody.

00:40:40.026 --> 00:40:43.957
So the self-esteem movement
I believe is super dangerous.

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:48.060
How are you gonna put World War II,

00:40:48.060 --> 00:40:52.150
get in a Higgins landing
craft and go hit Normandy.

00:40:52.150 --> 00:40:55.100
19-year-olds with super high self-esteem,

00:40:55.100 --> 00:40:56.960
you think they're getting in that thing?

00:40:56.960 --> 00:40:58.100
You think they're gonna

00:40:58.100 --> 00:41:00.410
rush a German pillbox?
- Oh yeah.

00:41:00.410 --> 00:41:01.700
- Hell no!

00:41:01.700 --> 00:41:02.533
No!

00:41:02.533 --> 00:41:04.760
So this thing that we thought was good,

00:41:04.760 --> 00:41:06.150
like hey, what if everyone

00:41:06.150 --> 00:41:08.713
just felt really good about themselves?

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:12.170
That's good if you earn it.

00:41:12.170 --> 00:41:15.030
If you just rub it on 'em,

00:41:15.030 --> 00:41:18.330
that ain't earning it and
that's gonna lead to trouble.

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:19.590
- So if we go a little meta on that,

00:41:19.590 --> 00:41:21.830
do you think that's actually by design?

00:41:21.830 --> 00:41:23.520
Do you think that is just like,

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:25.170
it's just like Liberals

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:27.750
that wanted everybody to
feel good and it's just nice,

00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:29.390
or do you think it was actually by design

00:41:29.390 --> 00:41:32.020
by some bad actors that
pushed this stuff out there

00:41:32.020 --> 00:41:36.010
to weaken all of the things
that the movie's about?

00:41:36.010 --> 00:41:39.030
- I think it can be traced back

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:42.700
to a gentleman who's out of Los Angeles

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:47.700
who had an idea which is it was,

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:49.710
and I'll paraphrase,

00:41:49.710 --> 00:41:52.290
but it's like some of
these inner-city kids

00:41:52.290 --> 00:41:54.400
and some of these low-income kids

00:41:54.400 --> 00:41:59.400
or single-parent kids and stuff,
they have low self-esteem.

00:41:59.480 --> 00:42:00.900
Their dad abandoned them.

00:42:00.900 --> 00:42:02.860
They don't feel good about themselves.

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:05.410
And if they felt good about themselves,

00:42:05.410 --> 00:42:07.620
they would start excelling in school

00:42:07.620 --> 00:42:11.520
and get outta gangs and
not be involved with crime,

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:15.480
so how do we get them to
feel good about themselves?

00:42:15.480 --> 00:42:16.313
And it's like,

00:42:16.313 --> 00:42:18.860
well, we can't get their
dad back in the house,

00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:21.500
and we can't give 'em an A in calculus

00:42:21.500 --> 00:42:23.380
when they're failing calculus,

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:26.170
we're just gonna start
telling 'em how good they are

00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:28.590
and they'll feel good about themselves.

00:42:28.590 --> 00:42:29.830
And what ended up happening

00:42:29.830 --> 00:42:33.230
is it turns out criminals
have high self-esteem.

00:42:33.230 --> 00:42:36.590
They didn't know it's a
unintended circumstance,

00:42:36.590 --> 00:42:38.510
like I have a good heart.

00:42:38.510 --> 00:42:41.960
It's basically all that
stuff of I have a good heart,

00:42:41.960 --> 00:42:44.440
let's build big housing projects

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:46.540
and let people live in free in those

00:42:46.540 --> 00:42:48.400
and we'll give 'em food and we'll level.

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:49.233
It's like, yeah,

00:42:49.233 --> 00:42:50.330
you're fooling them.
- All sounds good.

00:42:50.330 --> 00:42:52.490
- It all sounds good,
you have a great heart,

00:42:52.490 --> 00:42:54.750
you've now ruined a group of people.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:57.163
That's how you ruin people.

00:42:59.016 --> 00:43:01.110
You give them things without earning it.

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:02.700
You give them food without earning it,

00:43:02.700 --> 00:43:04.380
you give 'em housing without earning it,

00:43:04.380 --> 00:43:06.070
or you give them self-esteem
without earning it.

00:43:06.070 --> 00:43:08.630
If you don't earn it, it doesn't work,

00:43:08.630 --> 00:43:10.940
it morphs into something bad,

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:15.940
and then comes back in
a weird vitriolic way.

00:43:16.330 --> 00:43:18.520
- So one of the things I kept
thinking during the movie

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:20.280
is over the last couple of years,

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:21.650
since I've had all these people on.

00:43:21.650 --> 00:43:24.470
I mean, so many of the people
in the movie have been in here

00:43:24.470 --> 00:43:27.500
to discuss the very things
they're bringing up in the movie,

00:43:27.500 --> 00:43:29.853
I've often thought, man,
I am overblowing this?

00:43:30.840 --> 00:43:32.510
Am I just exaggerating?

00:43:32.510 --> 00:43:34.850
Or am I just finding these random people,

00:43:34.850 --> 00:43:36.300
Lindsay Shepard, this TA,

00:43:36.300 --> 00:43:38.500
Wilfrid Laurier University in Canada

00:43:38.500 --> 00:43:40.220
which I had never heard of before,

00:43:40.220 --> 00:43:41.450
am I overblowing this?

00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:44.280
And then the answer
always turned out to be no

00:43:44.280 --> 00:43:45.120
because the next week,

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:46.237
there's somebody else that it happens to,

00:43:46.237 --> 00:43:47.070
and the next week,

00:43:47.070 --> 00:43:49.740
there's somebody else and the rest of it.

00:43:49.740 --> 00:43:51.460
But were you ever worried about that,

00:43:51.460 --> 00:43:52.830
that as you make this,

00:43:52.830 --> 00:43:55.050
that it makes it feel like
it's bigger than it is?

00:43:55.050 --> 00:43:57.803
Or do you really believe
that it is this big?

00:43:59.200 --> 00:44:04.200
- I think when you endeavor to
do any kind of storytelling,

00:44:05.500 --> 00:44:10.500
there's a balance between
making it compelling.

00:44:14.150 --> 00:44:18.610
I'll give you an example
and then I'll circle back.

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:21.170
I've made other
documentaries about subjects

00:44:21.170 --> 00:44:22.610
that have nothing to do with this,

00:44:22.610 --> 00:44:25.940
and I made a documentary about Paul Newman

00:44:25.940 --> 00:44:28.397
and his racing life and it's called

00:44:28.397 --> 00:44:30.340
"Winning: The Racing Life of Paul Newman",

00:44:30.340 --> 00:44:33.393
and your audience would enjoy it.

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:38.540
His partner, his teammate,

00:44:38.540 --> 00:44:43.153
and his best friend and teammate
died at a race in Florida.

00:44:44.050 --> 00:44:46.483
Paul was racing, he was racing.

00:44:48.206 --> 00:44:50.293
Fitzie was his name, he died in the race.

00:44:51.450 --> 00:44:55.870
He died, they think, because
he had a heart attack

00:44:55.870 --> 00:44:57.720
when he was driving the car

00:44:57.720 --> 00:45:00.480
and just never slowed down for turn three

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:02.050
and just plowed in the walls.

00:45:02.050 --> 00:45:03.620
- Jeez.
- And died.

00:45:03.620 --> 00:45:06.083
And when you're making the film,

00:45:07.270 --> 00:45:10.540
I'm saying it's more dramatic

00:45:10.540 --> 00:45:13.880
if he just never hit
the brakes on turn three

00:45:13.880 --> 00:45:17.140
and gotten this horrific
accident and died.

00:45:17.140 --> 00:45:21.270
Now, the truth is, he had
a heart attack, probably,

00:45:21.270 --> 00:45:25.510
and died and then hit the
wall and died for sure

00:45:25.510 --> 00:45:27.540
or broke his neck or whatever it is.

00:45:27.540 --> 00:45:31.840
And as a filmmaker, I'm
like, I don't wanna lie

00:45:31.840 --> 00:45:36.360
but I'd like to make this
dramatic so I took out the part

00:45:36.360 --> 00:45:39.590
where he probably was
dead from the heart attack

00:45:39.590 --> 00:45:41.860
and just said he went
down the straightaway,

00:45:41.860 --> 00:45:43.630
he never braked for turn three,

00:45:43.630 --> 00:45:45.963
he died when he hit the wall.

00:45:45.963 --> 00:45:50.200
And so there's this thing
where it's like don't lie

00:45:50.200 --> 00:45:52.480
but you're also making a film.

00:45:52.480 --> 00:45:54.810
You're trying to entertain.

00:45:54.810 --> 00:45:58.860
I think what's going on on
college campuses is bad.

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:00.990
I have 13-year-old twins.

00:46:00.990 --> 00:46:04.130
I have reservations about
sending them to college,

00:46:04.130 --> 00:46:07.800
and I do hear the stories,
and I don't like the stories.

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:10.280
But by the same token,

00:46:10.280 --> 00:46:13.870
you're trying to make a film
that holds people's interest,

00:46:13.870 --> 00:46:16.260
and the music swells,

00:46:16.260 --> 00:46:18.820
and you take a little artistic license

00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:20.650
and it's a dramatic shot

00:46:20.650 --> 00:46:23.170
of the smoke burning in
slow motion and stuff.

00:46:23.170 --> 00:46:25.270
And yeah, so of course, there's an element

00:46:25.270 --> 00:46:27.020
of we are gonna massage it

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:33.180
like they do in theatrical
filmmaking as well.

00:46:33.700 --> 00:46:35.890
It's speaking to the
same party of the brain.

00:46:35.890 --> 00:46:39.500
You want people to sit up, pay
attention, and what-have-you,

00:46:39.500 --> 00:46:41.020
and it's just a part of filmmaking.

00:46:41.020 --> 00:46:42.870
So the answer is yes and yes.

00:46:42.870 --> 00:46:44.930
I do think it's bad but also,

00:46:44.930 --> 00:46:47.490
you take some artistic
license and ratchet it up.

00:46:47.490 --> 00:46:48.323
- Right.

00:46:48.323 --> 00:46:49.156
And you guys actually hit all that

00:46:49.156 --> 00:46:49.989
because there are moments

00:46:49.989 --> 00:46:53.100
where it's like my heart was tight,

00:46:53.100 --> 00:46:54.360
like oh man, this is horrible,

00:46:54.360 --> 00:46:55.790
and then a minute later, you're laughing.

00:46:55.790 --> 00:46:57.730
So it does kinda feel that.

00:46:57.730 --> 00:46:58.990
When you see people,

00:46:58.990 --> 00:47:01.220
I thought there's an extensive portion

00:47:01.220 --> 00:47:02.710
on what happened to Bret Weinstein

00:47:02.710 --> 00:47:06.860
and his wife Heather at Evergreen.

00:47:06.860 --> 00:47:07.850
And as I was watching it,

00:47:07.850 --> 00:47:10.310
and I know Bret very well
now and we're good friends,

00:47:10.310 --> 00:47:11.630
I was thinking like wow,

00:47:11.630 --> 00:47:14.650
how did he not see this coming

00:47:14.650 --> 00:47:17.840
as a Lefty professor at a Lefty school?

00:47:17.840 --> 00:47:20.430
Did you think that at all about
how did these guys not see

00:47:20.430 --> 00:47:23.540
that this thing was just
brewing all this time

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:26.380
until the day before the kids

00:47:26.380 --> 00:47:28.780
are showing up with the
baseball bats on campus?

00:47:34.264 --> 00:47:35.980
- I think there's a disconnect.

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:38.633
I've had this thought all the time.

00:47:47.090 --> 00:47:48.830
This is a big thing with Dr. Drew.

00:47:48.830 --> 00:47:52.803
It's like there's a whole
anti-smoking coalition.

00:47:54.720 --> 00:47:56.097
So there's a anti-smoking coalition,

00:47:56.097 --> 00:47:58.740
and we hate smoke, secondhand
smoke, third-hand smoke,

00:47:58.740 --> 00:48:01.960
there's no place for smoke
and then somebody goes,

00:48:01.960 --> 00:48:04.760
oh, okay, we now have vaping,

00:48:04.760 --> 00:48:07.420
it's just a vapor with nicotine

00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:09.590
which is inert with vapor

00:48:09.590 --> 00:48:13.030
and that's basically
saying caffeine and water.

00:48:13.030 --> 00:48:14.660
So good, we're happy now.

00:48:14.660 --> 00:48:18.020
They go, no, no, let's outlaw that.

00:48:18.020 --> 00:48:20.920
And you go, hold on, I
thought you didn't like smoke.

00:48:20.920 --> 00:48:22.340
It's like, yeah, no, we don't want.

00:48:22.340 --> 00:48:27.340
And you start to realize some
groups, like PETA's that way,

00:48:28.670 --> 00:48:31.100
the anti-smoking people are that way.

00:48:31.100 --> 00:48:33.350
There's a right version of that.

00:48:33.350 --> 00:48:36.910
When I did "Loveline", it
was like Christian coalition.

00:48:36.910 --> 00:48:39.330
They're against abortions.

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:40.380
Oh, okay, good.

00:48:40.380 --> 00:48:42.260
Now, they have the morning-after pill.

00:48:42.260 --> 00:48:44.420
And they go, yeah, we're against that too.

00:48:44.420 --> 00:48:47.240
And you go, wait a minute,
that's gonna stop abortion!

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:49.920
So they go, yeah, we're
not down with that pill.

00:48:49.920 --> 00:48:51.840
And you're like, wait, you hate abortions?

00:48:51.840 --> 00:48:52.673
Yes.

00:48:52.673 --> 00:48:53.780
There's a pill you can take

00:48:53.780 --> 00:48:55.730
that stops that insemination.

00:48:55.730 --> 00:48:57.690
It's like, yeah, we don't want that.

00:48:57.690 --> 00:48:58.790
Like, okay.

00:48:58.790 --> 00:49:01.270
So now I'm starting to
think you just want control.

00:49:01.270 --> 00:49:04.400
- My favorite one of those is Peter Thiel

00:49:04.400 --> 00:49:06.740
who's the openly gay billionaire.

00:49:06.740 --> 00:49:10.270
Out Magazine wrote a piece on
him that he's not gay actually

00:49:10.270 --> 00:49:12.010
because he's Conservative,

00:49:12.010 --> 00:49:14.250
and that would trump his gayness.

00:49:14.250 --> 00:49:15.111
- Right.
- They wanted to--

00:49:15.111 --> 00:49:17.650
- I love it when they do it
with gay or black or anything.

00:49:17.650 --> 00:49:19.810
Candace Owens, she's not
black anymore because--

00:49:19.810 --> 00:49:21.430
- Not black.
- I love it.

00:49:21.430 --> 00:49:23.030
I love the hypocrisy.

00:49:23.030 --> 00:49:26.670
And I realize that the group,

